<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cinematograph">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cinematograph</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as kinematograph, Cinématographe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The cinematograph is an early motion-picture machine that served as both film camera and projector; the French inventor Léon Bouly coined the name in a patent of 12 February 1892, and filed for a version that could also project in December 1893. The Lumière brothers filed a patent for their machine, which could also print films, on 13 February 1895, and Louis Lumière first presented a film in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 22 March 1895. After their public screening in December 1895 it became a popular attraction across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cinematograph.jpg" alt="Cinematograph" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcellin Auzolle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cinematographe_Lumiere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> was introduced in 1839, people saw that its images could be combined with the principle of the phenakistiscope, but real-time recording of motion took several decades. Eadweard Muybridge's chronophotography of 1878 inspired many attempts, and in 1887 Ottomar Anschütz introduced his Elektrische Schnellseher, which showed chronophotographic sequences on a small glass screen to paying audiences.</p><p>Léon Bouly took the name Cinématographe from the Greek for writing in movement. Turning a handle made a padded pressure plate stop the film intermittently as a rotating cylinder carried it. At least three Bouly cinematographs were built, but his patents lapsed at the end of 1894 because the annual fees were paid only once, and he seems to have abandoned the project.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Lumière brothers wanted a machine as simple and economical as a common photographic camera, since many of their customers were amateur photographers. Their Cinématographe weighed only 16 lb (7.3 kg), unlike the bulky, electrically powered Kinetoscope. The same device took pictures, printed positives from the negative and projected them. A common magic lantern made by Alfred Molteni served for the projections of 1895. The film was 35 mm, with one perforation on each side of each frame, and the Lumières advocated 15 frames per second, usually recording about 50 seconds of action in 800 pictures.</p><p>Louis Lumière decided by February 1896 on a fixed shutter and a lens diaphragm after noticing that perfectly sharp pictures looked jerky in sudden movement, and in 1897 the Lumières added a glass flask of water as condenser, which also protected the flammable film.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Louis Lumière presented his first film, Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory, on 22 March 1895 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to a few dozen invited guests, who were more enthusiastic about it than about the colour slides expected to be the main item. The first Lumière public show was on 28 December 1895 at the Salon Indien du Grand Café, with ten short films each about 17 metres long. The first commercial public screening had in fact been held on 20 May 1895 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> with the Eidoloscope of Woodville Latham and Eugene Lauste.</p><p>The Cinématographe became a popular attraction for people of all classes. It was shown in nickelodeons, at fairs and in vaudeville houses, and the Lumières took it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its reversibility was abandoned after a few years, because few amateurs made films and theatrical screenings grew popular, so specialised cameras and projectors were wanted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cinematograph” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At a meeting in Paris, Louis and Auguste Lumière projected 'Workers Leaving the Lumière Factory' with their <b>Cinématographe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1892</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Léon Bouly · Lumière brothers</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The Lumière brothers’ machine of 1895 gave the famous early screenings in Paris; the first commercial screening of films took place in New York in May 1895.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/photography">Photography<span>1826–1827</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cinematograph">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
